WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.840
In my Daily Dozen checklist,

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:07.040
I recommend eating one
tablespoon of flaxseed a day.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.920
But you may have heard
that flaxseed contains cyanide.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.520
What?!

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.920
Is it anything to worry about?
Watch the video to find out.

00:00:16.034 --> 00:00:22.753
"How Well Does Cooking Destroy Cyanide
in Flax Seeds and Should We Be Concerned?"

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.400
Sweden's dietary guidelines
are pioneering in many ways;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.840
for example, encouraging people
to decrease their climate impact

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.120
by choosing more plant-based foods,
which tend to produce greatly

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.680
fewer greenhouse gas emissions.
But I was surprised by this page

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.640
on the official Swedish
National Food Agency website,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.120
which translates to cyanogenic
glycosides and hydrogen cyanide,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.120
encouraging people to stay away
from ground flaxseed

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.360
for fear of cyanide toxicity,
as in the ground flax seeds

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.400
I encourage everyone
to eat every day.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.760
You can see why this was
the first question I got

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.040
when I spoke in Stockholm.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.960
Was the Swedish government
on to something?

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.720
Had I just been duped by
Big Flax-funded researchers

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.040
who claimed you could eat pounds of
ground flaxseeds a day without worrying,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.400
more than 150 tablespoons a day?
First, some background.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.880
As many as one in five plants
that we eat produce cyanide.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.520
In fact, if you look at the major
food crops in the world,

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.720
more than half are cyanogenic,
meaning cyanide producing.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.360
See, unlike toxic elements like lead,
mercury, arsenic that can't be broken

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.080
down into anything, cyanide
is an organic molecule:

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.520
one carbon atom attached
to one nitrogen atom.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.840
In this state it can definitely be toxic,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.640
but broken down or complexed to
something else and it loses its toxicity.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.000
And we have an enzyme in our
body that does just that,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.400
a cyanide detoxifying enzyme.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.280
And that's just one of five main ways
our body can detoxify cyanide.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.120
It does require protein to do it though,
and so that's why you can read about

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.200
chronic cyanide toxicity among
malnourished populations in Africa

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.560
trying to live off of improperly
processed cassava root.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.360
But as long as we're getting
adequate protein in our diet,

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.520
our body can detoxify the normal
amounts of cyanide we eat every day.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.320
Now there is a rare, congenital genetic
condition called Leber's disease,

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.600
where you're born without
the ability to detoxify cyanide.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.280
And indeed, yeah, you could go blind
drinking apple cider or something,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.240
but otherwise our bodies evolved to
be cyanide-detoxifying machines,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.320
but obviously there's a limit.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.800
For example, a case of cyanide
poisoning after bitter almond ingestion.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.160
Not regular almonds, which produce
about 40 times less cyanide,

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.720
but bitter almonds, which you can't even
buy—they're used in flavor manufacturing—

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.000
but if you did, eating 50
of them could kill you,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.720
or even just a handful
for a small child.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.640
This suggests that eating 2,000
regular almonds at one sitting

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.000
could also be bad news.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.240
You can't buy bitter almonds,
but you can buy apricots,

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.520
and apricot kernels, which are the
seeds inside the stone, actually have

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.960
pretty toxic levels, and have
indeed been implicated in cases

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.920
of severe cyanide poisoning,
all linked to the

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.160
"Laetrile: cult of cyanide,
Promoting poison for profit"

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.600
that I talked about previously.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.280
So I'm totally sympathetic to regulators
wanting to take a precautionary approach.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.440
But are flaxseeds like bitter almonds,
where just a few ounces could kill you,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.480
or more like regular almonds
where regular dietary intake

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.440
wouldn't even come close?

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.360
Although the fact that flaxseeds
can produce cyanide sounds like

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.040
it would be a significant
health concern,

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.760
it is not for several reasons, says
flax industry-funded scientists.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.200
First, the adult human body
has the ability to detoxify

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.480
up to 100 mg of cyanide per day.
That's where they come up with

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.200
their "pounds of flaxseeds
a day are safe" number.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.280
And if you wanted
to eat more than

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:59.920
those totally unrealistic
150 tablespoons a day,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.440
you could just eat them in baked goods
since cooking destroys the cyanide.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.560
And eating 7 or 8 tablespoons
of raw doesn't even bump

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.320
urinary thiocyanate levels, which is
an indicator of cyanide exposure,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.800
so it doesn't even look like
your body is even exposed to it.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.240
Thus, the toxicity of flaxseed
from cyanogenic glycosides

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.680
is not, evidently,
a realistic health threat.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.560
OK, let's unpack that.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.120
The cooking part is mostly true.
Yes, bake muffins with even

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.800
like a quarter cup of ground flax
each for 15 to 18 minutes

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.720
at about 450 degrees Fahrenheit and
the cyanide-forming compounds are gone.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.680
Looks like the same thing
happens with baked bread.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.840
But bake ground flax seeds on their
own, and even an hour at 350

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.400
only wipes out 20%, though baking
them whole wipes out 80%.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.280
And what, baking in bread
or muffins wipes out 100%?

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.080
How does that make any sense?

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.520
It's the moisture.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.000
It's heat plus water
that wipes out the cyanide.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.280
Boiling for just five minutes can
wipe it out, like when making

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.720
hot cereal or something.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.800
And so, yes, it's true
in most cases

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.200
that cooking eliminates
the cyanide compounds

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.480
because it typically starts out
in a batter as an egg substitute,

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.680
or baking crackers, or the
dough starts out moist.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.600
And so, yes, in those cases the
cyanide is gone upon cooking.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.720
But you can't just spread ground
flaxseeds on a baking sheet

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.280
because they dry out so fast that
only a minority of the cyanide is lost.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.960
But, look, why does it
matter if your body

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.840
doesn't even seem to notice
7 or 8 tablespoons of raw?

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:39.840
But that's not true.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.120
Urinary thiocyanate
excretion doubled at that level,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.880
though that's just a sign your
body is actively detoxifying it.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.480
And if we can detoxify a kilo worth
of flax a day, what's the problem?

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.680
Well, look, even if the adult human
body has the ability to detoxify

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.760
up to 100 mg of cyanide a day,
first of all, look, kids eat flax too.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.920
Second of all, a kilo has more than
the 100 you said we could detoxify,

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.440
about 50% more.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.040
And I'm not interested in how
much we can detoxify "up to."

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.080
For safety you're interested
in the worst-case scenario,

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.760
not the best-case scenario.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:23.120
Would someone please just give
people different doses of flaxseeds

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.680
and just measure how much
cyanide ends up in their blood?

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:33.840
But that's never been done until
now, which we'll cover next.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.600
Flaxseeds pack a nutritional punch.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.480
And as a bonus the release
of cyanide from flaxseed

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:48.320
is below a toxic lethal dose.
Well, I should hope so.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.560
Back-of-the-envelope type
calculations have led

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.920
industry-funded scientists to assert
that a person would have to consume

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:59.360
8 cups of ground flaxseed at a time
to achieve acute cyanide toxicity.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.120
I'd feel better though if it
was actually put to the test.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:07.840
Researchers tested flaxseeds under
worst-case scenario conditions,

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.960
with respect to resulting in higher
cyanide levels in the blood.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.880
So (1) locate the flaxseed
with the highest level

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.280
of cyanide-forming
compounds you can find.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:18.400
So they went to stores

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.600
and bought 15 different sources of
flaxseed, and though the average level

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.880
was 140 milligrams per kilo,
which is about typical,

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.000
they did find one with 220,
so they used that one.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.720
(2) maximal mechanical destruction
to release the most cyanide.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.840
So they used some crazy
20,000 RPM lab grinder.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:40.080
(3) eat it all at once
on an empty stomach,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.440
and then keep the stomach empty.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.760
And they gave it raw, since cooking
can often wipe it all out.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:49.600
If the recommended daily dose
is like one or two tablespoons

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:53.440
of ground flaxseed a day—I recommend
one in my Daily Dozen checklist—

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:56.560
they decided to go with four
and a half tablespoons.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:58.720
OK, so what happened?

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.840
The range of cyanide blood levels
that one might estimate to possibly

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.240
be associated with the clinical symptoms
of intoxication would be like 20 to 40.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.400
So that would be like here or higher,
where we want to stay below.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.760
So four and a half tablespoons
on an empty stomach

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.440
of the highest cyanide-containing ultra-
ground raw flaxseeds they could find,

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:26.160
and the highest individual
level rise was just under 14,

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:27.920
and the average was down around 6.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:32.640
There has to be some amount of flax
that takes you over the limit though.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.160
So, they tested 9 tablespoons
and 15 tablespoons too.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.840
Remember, we start to
worry at around 20 to 40.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.400
Three and a half teaspoons of raw high-
cyanide ground flax on an empty stomach?

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.200
Hardly a blip.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.640
Seven teaspoons at a time?
Same thing.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.400
Fourteen teaspoons, which is four and
a half tablespoons, and there's that 6.

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.920
OK, but what about a little
over 9 tablespoons—

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.600
that's over a half cup at a time—
and that does start skirting toxicity.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.400
And finally, what about
a whole cup?

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.560
I don't even know how you'd
eat a whole cup at once,

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.720
but that is too much, putting you
in that potential toxic range

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:11.680
for about three hours.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.840
So much for the industry's
8 cups at a time are safe.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.880
But even in this worst-case
scenario situation, one cup raw

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.360
on an empty stomach at the
highest dose they could find,

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.280
that person still didn't actually
have any clinical symptoms.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:32.480
This is consistent with the fact that
there's not a single published report

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.840
of cyanide poisoning after consumption
of flaxseeds anywhere in the literature,

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.640
even from Swedish health spas,

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.920
where they evidently give up to
12 tablespoons as a fiber shock.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:46.720
Usually high doses are like two or
so tablespoons three times a day,

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:51.360
and this dose would be safe with respect
to possible acute toxicity of cyanide.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.240
OK, but what about any
possible chronic toxicity?

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:58.720
The World Health Organization
has something called

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:03.360
a PMTDI, the provisional
maximum tolerable daily intake.

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:06.160
It's defined as the amount you
can eat safely, every day,

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:09.440
for the rest of your life,
without risking any adverse

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.080
health effects, based on
the best available data,

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:15.360
though often that's just like
rat studies as it was in this case.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.200
If you put varying doses of cyanide
in the drinking water of rats

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.560
for a few months, at a certain level,
the so-called benchmark dose

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.920
lower confidence limit, there's
a 10% increased incidence

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.520
of the shrinkage of the tail of
the epididymis, which is where

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.960
sperm is stored in the testicles.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.280
That happens at the human equivalent
of about 150 tablespoons

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.440
of flaxseeds a day worth of cyanide.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:38.880
But they want to err
on the side of caution,

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:44.320
so they introduce a 100-fold uncertainty
factor to create the PMTDI.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.960
So instead of 150 tablespoons
of flaxseeds a day,

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:50.800
the average American should stick
to under 1.5 tablespoons a day,

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.240
if you're going to eat
them every day.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:55.920
So my tablespoon-a-day Daily Dozen
recommendation should be safe

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.840
by any of these standards.

